{{also|at-large}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|at-large}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|at large|t=at liberty or freedom}} (compare {{cog|enm|ben at large|t=to be at one's liberty, be free}}). Compare {{cog|fro|au large}} (“at liberty” and other senses).

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-at large.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Prepositional phrase===
{{en-prep phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[on the loose|On the loose]]; roaming freely; not [[confined]].
#: ''For a nervous twenty-four hours, three wanted criminals were '''at large''' in the city.''
#: ''The ambassador-'''at-large''' was designated to the Middle East as a region, rather than to a specific country.''
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[in full|In full]], [[fully]].
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy}}, vol.I, New York 2001, p.236:
#*: The like example I find in Lælius à Fonte Eugubinus, ''consult.'' 129&nbsp;[…]. Read in him the story '''at large'''.
# [[in general|In general]]; [[as a whole]].
#: ''Some people support the measure, but the community '''at large''' will probably be against it.''
# {{lb|en|US|politics|of an election}} Having an [[electorate]] across multiple [[district]]s.
#: ''The city has five city council districts; however, the mayor is elected '''at large'''.''

